<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Pitcairn Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1838 CE – present · capital Adamstown</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's smallest and most remote overseas territory, settled by the Bounty mutineers and their Tahitian companions in 1790.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fletcher-christian">Fletcher Christian</a> and eight other mutineers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-bounty">HMS Bounty</a>, with Tahitian men and women, landed on uninhabited Pitcairn in 1790 and burned the ship. Violence killed most of the men, and the survivors were discovered in 1808. In 1838 Captain Russell Elliott of HMS Fly drew up laws that made the island a British possession and gave women the vote.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The tiny community of mixed English and Tahitian descent was governed by an elected magistrate and council under British oversight, latterly through the High Commissioner in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. The islanders converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventh-day-adventist-church">Seventh-day Adventism</a> in the 1890s. They live by fishing, gardening, selling handicrafts and stamps to passing ships, and they speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pitkern">Pitkern</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> of English and Tahitian.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The whole population moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk-island">Norfolk Island</a> in 1856, but some families returned from 1858. Pitcairn remains a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territory</a> with about 40 to 50 residents, shaken in 2004 by trials for historical sexual abuse.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pitcairn-islands.jpg" alt="Pitcairn Islands" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: User:Dbenbenn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Pitcairn_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1838 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Adamstown</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Seventh-day Adventism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Pitkern</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pitcairn</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=pitcairn-islands" aria-label="Pitcairn Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1230 3100 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pitcairn Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M1550 3356L4997 651M1550 3356L4997 651"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M1550 3356h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M1550 3356h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Adamstown. <a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=pitcairn-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

